Glad you're here. Welcome to Models &amp; Agents Episode 85 for June 19, 2026.

00:00:06.180 --> 00:00:12.320
Your daily AI briefing. Your daily briefing on the AI models and agents that are changing everything.

00:00:13.000 --> 00:00:16.740
And no, not those kinds of models and agents. Let's get into it.

00:00:17.340 --> 00:00:22.460
Nvidia just released an open frontier model built from the ground up for long-running agents.

00:00:23.020 --> 00:00:29.840
Nvidia released Nemotron 3 Ultra, an open frontier model positioned for long-running AI agents.

00:00:30.340 --> 00:00:35.300
The model targets sustained agent operation rather than single-turned-chat,

00:00:35.920 --> 00:00:41.220
addressing a gap where most open models still struggle with extended tool use loops and

00:00:41.220 --> 00:00:47.720
state management. Builders working on autonomous workflows now have a high capability open option

00:00:47.720 --> 00:00:51.840
that can be self-hosted or fine-tuned without closed model restrictions.

00:00:52.780 --> 00:00:58.080
Early positioning suggests it competes directly with the top closed models on agent relevant

00:00:58.080 --> 00:01:05.620
tasks while remaining fully open weights. Watch for integration examples with existing agent frameworks

00:01:05.620 --> 00:01:10.660
and any announced context length or tool calling benchmarks in the coming weeks.

00:01:11.500 --> 00:01:17.120
DeepSeek previewed the V4 series with two MOE models that support million token

00:01:17.120 --> 00:01:27.740
contexts. V4 Pro totals 1.6 trillion parameters with 49 billion active while V4 Flash totals 284

00:01:27.740 --> 00:01:35.780
billion parameters with 13 billion active. Both use hybrid compressed sparse attention plus heavily

00:01:35.780 --> 00:01:42.980
compressed attention, manifold constrained hyperconnections, and the muon optimizer after pre-training

00:01:42.980 --> 00:01:52.880
on over 32 trillion tokens. V4 Pro Max reportedly cuts inference plops to 27% and KV cash to 10%

00:01:52.880 --> 00:01:58.540
of the prior version at million token lengths. Checkpoints are already on hugging face.

00:01:59.160 --> 00:02:03.600
This moves the open weight model's conversation forward from last week's coverage,

00:02:04.080 --> 00:02:08.660
where the performance gap with closed systems remained the central open question.

00:02:09.540 --> 00:02:14.580
Liquid AI released two 350 million parameter retriever models.

00:02:14.980 --> 00:02:20.480
The pair includes a dense buying coder and a call-burt late interaction variant.

00:02:21.060 --> 00:02:28.020
Both target fast multilingual search across 11 languages. The models focus on edge deployment

00:02:28.020 --> 00:02:34.520
with low latency retrieval on resource constrained hardware. They combine dense and late interaction

00:02:34.520 --> 00:02:41.400
approaches to balance speed and accuracy. Developers building on-device rag or search features

00:02:41.400 --> 00:02:45.740
now have a compact multilingual option that avoids larger cloud models.

00:02:46.600 --> 00:02:51.700
The releases follow Liquid AI's earlier work on efficient inference architectures.

